To achieve this, you can use JavaScript (preferably with JQuery for simpler syntax) to dynamically load content into a central area of your webpage when tabs are clicked, without reloading the entire page (this is the essence of AJAX). Additionally, for the initial load, you can set the Quizzes content to be displayed by default. 

Here’s a step-by-step guide on how to implement tabs with AJAX content loading:

### Step 1: Prepare your HTML Structure

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Dynamic Content Tabs</title>
    <!-- Optional: Include CSS for styling -->
</head>
<body>
    <div id="tabs">
        <ul>
            <li><a href="#" id="quizzes-tab">Quizzes</a></li>
            <li><a href="#" id="study-guides-tab">Study Guides</a></li>
            <li><a href="#" id="video-tutorials-tab">Video Tutorials</a></li>
        </ul>
    </div>
    <div id="content-area">
        <!-- Content will be loaded here -->
    </div>

    <!-- Load JQuery (you can also download and host it locally) -->
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
    <script src="script.js"></script> <!-- Load your JavaScript file -->
</body>
</html>
```

### Step 2: Create the JavaScript file (script.js)

First, let’s make sure to load the "Quizzes" content by default when the page loads. Then, you’ll add click event listeners for each tab that will load the respective content using AJAX.

```javascript
$(document).ready(function() {
    // Load quizzes content by default
    loadContent('quizzes');

    // Bind click events
    $('#quizzes-tab').click(function(e) {
        e.preventDefault();
        loadContent('quizzes');
    });

    $('#study-guides-tab').click(function(e) {
        e.preventDefault();
        loadContent('studyGuides');
    });

    $('#video-tutorials-tab').click(function(e) {
        e.preventDefault();
        loadContent('videoTutorials');
    });

    // Function to load content
    function loadContent(type) {
        // Example: Load content through AJAX
        $.ajax({
            url: type + '.html', // Assuming you have separate .html files for content
            method: 'GET',
            success: function(response) {
                $('#content-area').html(response);
            },
            error: function() {
                $('#content-area').html('<p>Error loading content.</p>');
            }
        });
    }
});
```

### Notes:

- Replace `type + '.html'` in the AJAX `url` parameter with your actual URLs or backend endpoints where your quizzes, study guides, and video tutorial content are stored.
- This code assumes you have separate HTML files (`quizzes.html`, `studyGuides.html`, `videoTutorials.html`) for each content type. Alternatively, if your content is delivered through an API, you may need to modify the `url` and potentially how the `success` function handles the response.
- Ensure JQuery is correctly loaded in your HTML file before `script.js`. 

This basic example can be further extended and customized to fit your needs. You can style the tabs with CSS and enhance the user experience by adding loading indicators or animations when switching between tabs.

import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { useDrag, useDrop } from 'react-dnd';
import { ItemTypes } from './ItemTypes';

const style = {
  border: '1px solid gray',
  padding: '0.5rem 1rem',
  marginBottom: '.5rem',
  backgroundColor: 'white',
  cursor: 'move',
};

const Element = ({ id, text, moveElement }) => {
  const [{ isDragging }, drag] = useDrag({
    item: { type: ItemTypes.ELEMENT, id, text },
    collect: (monitor) => ({
      isDragging: monitor.isDragging(),
    }),
  });
  const [, drop] = useDrop({
    accept: ItemTypes.ELEMENT,
    drop: (item) => {
      moveElement(item.id, id);
    },
  });

  return (
    <div ref={(node) => drag(drop(node))} style={{ ...style, opacity: isDragging ? 0.5 : 1 }}>
      {text}
    </div>
  );
};

const Container = ({ elements, moveElement }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      {elements.map((element, index) => (
        <Element key={element.id} id={element.id} text={element.text} moveElement={moveElement} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

const App = () => {
  const [elements1, setElements1] = useState([
    { id: 1, text: 'Element 1' },
    { id: 2, text: 'Element 2' },
    { id: 3, text: 'Element 3' },
  ]);
  const [elements2, setElements2] = useState([
    { id: 4, text: 'Element 4' },
    { id: 5, text: 'Element 5' },
    { id: 6, text: 'Element 6' },
  ]);

  const moveElement = (id, atIndex) => {
    const element = elements1.find((e) => e.id === id);
    const newElements1 = elements1.filter((e) => e.id !== id);
    setElements1(newElements1);
    setElements2((prevElements2) => [...prevElements2.slice(0, atIndex), element, ...prevElements2.slice(atIndex)]);
  };

  return (
    <div style={{ display: 'flex' }}>
      <Container elements={elements1} moveElement={moveElement} />
      <Container elements={elements2} moveElement={moveElement} />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;

Este código utiliza las funciones y del paquete para lograr la funcionalidad de arrastrar y soltar elementos entre divs. Hay dos contenedores que tienen use Draguse Dropreact- dnd Container
dentro de ellos una matriz de elementos, y cada elemento es representado por un componente . El componente utiliza y para definir su comportamiento de arrastrar y soltar. Element Element use Draguse Drop

En el componente , hay dos estados y que almacenan los elementos en cada contenedor. La función se utiliza para mover un elemento de un contenedor a otro.Appelements1elements2moveElement

Espero que esto te ayude a comprender cómo se puede implementar la funcionalidad de arrastrar y soltar elementos en React.

